  "title": "Al --- Two Words Spanish Will Not Keep Apart",
  "drivablePrefix": 1,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:cbace5daee5ed0c1",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C61-al",
      "sequence": 2385,
      "title": "al — two words Spanish will not let you keep apart",
      "headword": "al",
      "romanization": "al",
      "gloss": "to the — one of only two places where Spanish forces two words into one, and it does it because the two vowels refuse to sit next to each other",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d3a84e46b8fee7ec",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have a — to, towards. And you have el."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put them together and say it quickly. A el... a el... Hear what your mouth wants to do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the squeeze",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "a + el becomes al."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Voy al café. — I'm going to the café."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is not an option and not a shortcut for speaking fast. It is how the language is written. A el café is not a more careful version of al café; it is simply wrong."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You met this instinct two chapters ago, when mucho lost its ending in front of an adjective. Spanish does not like a word standing about doing nothing while another word waits: when one word leans on the next, something gives. Here the two words simply fuse."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And there is one exception worth knowing, because it looks like a mistake and is not. When El is part of a name, it keeps its distance:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Voy a El Salvador. — not al Salvador."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What contracts is the little article — not every word that happens to be spelled el."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "al café, al profesor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *al café*, *al profesor*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Voy al café",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Voy al café*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C61-al-say",
              "prompt": "Say: I'm going to the cafe.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-CONTRACTION-AL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "voy al cafe",
                "voy al cafe."
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Two words, one shape.",
                "incorrect": "Voy al cafe."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C61-al-must",
              "prompt": "Is a el cafe a more careful way of saying it?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-CONTRACTION-OBLIGATORY"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 13,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "no",
                "no, it is simply wrong"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The contraction is the written form.",
                "incorrect": "No -- a el cafe is wrong, not formal."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C61-al-name",
              "prompt": "Why does Voy a El Salvador keep the two words apart?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-CONTRACTION-AL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "el is part of the name",
                "because el is part of a name"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The rule is about the article, not the spelling.",
                "incorrect": "Because El there is part of a name."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A plus el makes what? (Al — always.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And when does it not happen? (When El is part of a name.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
